{"name":"River God","hex":"#6c6c5f","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"river god","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The tutelary deity of a river."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/river_god"}],"entries":[{"title":"River gods (Greek mythology)","blocks":[{"text":"In ancient Greek religion and mythology, rivers were often personified as deities, and in a number of ancient Greek cities river gods were the subject of local worship. In Hesiod's Theogony, the river gods are the offspring of the Titans Oceanus and Tethys, and the brothers of the Oceanids. In Greek mythology, river deities – such as Inachus, Scamander, and Peneus – are often progenitors of local genealogical lines."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/6/6d/%D0%91%D0%BE%D0%B3_%D1%80%D0%B5%D0%BA%D0%B8_%D0%9D%D0%B8%D0%BB_%D0%BA%D0%BE%D0%BF%D1%82%D1%81%D0%BA%D0%B0%D1%8F_%D1%82%D0%BA%D0%B0%D0%BD%D1%8C_IV_%D0%92.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/River_gods_(Greek_mythology)"},{"title":"List of water deities","blocks":[{"text":"A water deity is a deity in mythology associated with water or various bodies of water. Water deities are common in mythology and were usually more important among civilizations in which the sea or ocean, or a great river was more important. Another important focus of worship of water deities has been springs or holy wells."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/12/Gaziantep_Zeugma_Museum_Water_gods_mosaic_8199.jpg/330px-Gaziantep_Zeugma_Museum_Water_gods_mosaic_8199.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_water_deities"},{"title":"River God","blocks":[{"text":"River God is a novel by author Wilbur Smith. It tells the story of the talented eunuch slave named Taita, his life in Egypt, the flight of Taita along with the Egyptian populace from the Hyksos invasion, and their eventual return. The novel can be grouped together with Wilbur Smith's other books on Ancient Egypt. It was first published in 1993, and was adapted for television alongside The Seventh Scroll as the 1999 mini-series The Seventh Scroll."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/8/84/River-god-cover.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/River_God"},{"title":"River","blocks":[{"text":"A river is a natural stream of fresh water that flows on land or inside caves towards another body of water at a lower elevation, such as an ocean, lake, or another river. A river may run dry before reaching the end of its course if it runs out of water, or only flow during certain seasons. Rivers are regulated by the water cycle, the processes by which water moves around the Earth. Water first enters rivers through precipitation, whether from rainfall, the runoff of water down a slope, the melting of glaciers or snow, or seepage from aquifers beneath the surface of the Earth."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/77/Pirogue_running_on_the_Mekong_at_golden_hour_between_Don_Det_and_Don_Khon_Laos.jpg/330px-Pirogue_running_on_the_Mekong_at_golden_hour_between_Don_Det_and_Don_Khon_Laos.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/River"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a3/%E9%B9%BF%E6%B8%AF%E6%96%B0%E7%A5%96%E5%AE%AE%E3%80%8C%E6%85%B6%E7%AB%AF%E9%99%BD%E6%B4%BB%E5%8B%95%E3%80%8D.jpg/500px-%E9%B9%BF%E6%B8%AF%E6%96%B0%E7%A5%96%E5%AE%AE%E3%80%8C%E6%85%B6%E7%AB%AF%E9%99%BD%E6%B4%BB%E5%8B%95%E3%80%8D.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a3/%E9%B9%BF%E6%B8%AF%E6%96%B0%E7%A5%96%E5%AE%AE%E3%80%8C%E6%85%B6%E7%AB%AF%E9%99%BD%E6%B4%BB%E5%8B%95%